/* enetxt.map - the linker version script for the shipped shared library.
 *
 * WHY: enetxt statically links ENet, and a static archive's symbols inherit
 * the shared library's default (public) visibility, so the shipped .so used to
 * export ~65 upstream enet_* symbols alongside our 22 enx_* ABI entry points.
 * That is a symbol-collision surface: another extension in the same engine
 * process that also bundles ENet (a different version, even) could interpose,
 * and whichever library the dynamic loader resolved first would win. The
 * engine's dlopen is typically RTLD_LOCAL, which makes this latent rather than
 * live, but "latent" is not a guarantee we want to ship.
 *
 * -fvisibility=hidden on our own target cannot fix it: ENet is compiled by its
 * own CMake target, so our flags never reach it. A version script filters at
 * LINK time and therefore does reach it.
 *
 * The public surface is exactly the enx_* C ABI the .lcb binds to by name;
 * everything else - ENet, the C++ runtime bits, our internal helpers - is
 * local. Adding a new entry point means adding an enx_* symbol, which this
 * pattern already covers; nothing here needs editing per handler.
 *
 * GNU ld / lld only (Linux). The Windows lane controls exports with a .def
 * file and macOS uses default visibility, so CMakeLists applies this only
 * where the linker supports it.
 */
{
    global:
        enx_*;
    local:
        *;
};
